<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#tui-lau">Glossary</a> · Term · title</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tui Lau</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tui Lau is a Fijian chiefly title meaning paramount chief of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lau-islands">Lau Islands</a> in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fiji">Fiji</a>. It was recognised for the Tongan noble <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/enele-maafu">Enele Maʻafu</a>, the dominant power in Lau, when Fiji was ceded to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> in 1874. Since then it has been held by Fijian high chiefs, most notably <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ratu-sir-kamisese-mara">Ratu Sir Kamisese Mara</a>, the first prime minister of independent Fiji.</p></div>
